**Postman uses JavaScript for writing tests. Here's a complete guide:**

**Basic Test Structure**
```javascript
pm.test('Test name', function() {
    // Assertion goes here
    pm.expect(actual).to.equal(expected);
});
```

**Common Test Examples:**

**1. Status Code Tests**
```javascript
// Single status code
pm.test('Status code is 200', () => {
    pm.response.to.have.status(200);
});

// Status code category
pm.test('Status code is 2xx', () => {
    pm.expect(pm.response.code).to.be.within(200, 299);
});

// Multiple possible status codes
pm.test('Status code is either 200 or 201', () => {
    pm.expect(pm.response.code).to.be.oneOf([200, 201]);
});
```

**2. Response Time Tests**
```javascript
pm.test('Response time < 500ms', () => {
    pm.expect(pm.response.responseTime).to.be.below(500);
});

pm.test('Response time is acceptable', () => {
    pm.expect(pm.response.responseTime).to.be.within(100, 1000);
});
```

**3. Header Tests**
```javascript
pm.test('Content-Type header exists', () => {
    pm.response.to.have.header('Content-Type');
});

pm.test('Content-Type is JSON', () => {
    pm.response.to.have.header('Content-Type', 'application/json');
});
```

**4. JSON Body Tests**
```javascript
const response = pm.response.json();

// Property exists
pm.test('Response has id property', () => {
    pm.expect(response).to.have.property('id');
});

// Multiple required fields
pm.test('Response has all required fields', () => {
    const required = ['id', 'name', 'email'];
    required.forEach(field => {
        pm.expect(response).to.have.property(field);
    });
});

// Data type validation
pm.test('id is a number', () => {
    pm.expect(response.id).to.be.a('number');
});

pm.test('name is a non-empty string', () => {
    pm.expect(response.name).to.be.a('string').and.not.empty;
});

// Value validation
pm.test('name matches expected', () => {
    pm.expect(response.name).to.equal('John Doe');
});

// Array tests
pm.test('users array has 10 items', () => {
    pm.expect(response.users).to.have.lengthOf(10);
});

pm.test('all users have valid email', () => {
    response.users.forEach(user => {
        pm.expect(user.email).to.match(/^[^@]+@[^@]+\.[^@]+$/);
    });
});
```

**5. Nested Property Tests**
```javascript
pm.test('address.city exists', () => {
    pm.expect(response).to.have.nested.property('address.city');
});

pm.test('address.zip is 5 digits', () => {
    pm.expect(response.address.zip).to.match(/^\d{5}$/);
});
```

**6. Schema Validation**
```javascript
const schema = {
    type: 'object',
    required: ['id', 'name'],
    properties: {
        id: { type: 'number' },
        name: { type: 'string' }
    }
};

pm.test('Response matches schema', () => {
    pm.expect(pm.response.json()).to.matchSchema(schema);
});
```

**7. Business Logic Tests**
```javascript
// Compare with request data
const requestData = JSON.parse(pm.request.body.raw);
const responseData = pm.response.json();

pm.test('Created resource matches request', () => {
    pm.expect(responseData.name).to.equal(requestData.name);
    pm.expect(responseData.email).to.equal(requestData.email);
});

// Calculated values
pm.test('Total price matches sum', () => {
    const sum = responseData.items.reduce((acc, item) => acc + item.price, 0);
    pm.expect(responseData.total).to.equal(sum);
});
```

**8. Saving Data for Next Tests**
```javascript
// Save user ID for subsequent requests
const userId = pm.response.json().id;
pm.environment.set('userId', userId);
```

**Test Results:**
- Tests run automatically after request
- Results shown in "Test Results" tab
- Green check = pass, Red X = fail
- Console.log for debugging

**Best Practices:**
- Use descriptive test names
- Test one thing per test
- Handle both success and error cases
- Use variables for dynamic values
- Save useful data for next requests

**Postman supports multiple authentication methods. Here's how to use each:**

**1. No Auth**
For public APIs that don't require authentication.
- Select "No Auth" in Auth tab

**2. API Key**
```
Method 1: Header
- Auth tab → Type: API Key
- Key: X-API-Key (or whatever API expects)
- Value: {{apiKey}}
- Add to: Header

Method 2: Query Parameter
- Auth tab → Type: API Key
- Key: api_key
- Value: {{apiKey}}
- Add to: Query Params

Method 3: Manual header
- Headers tab → Add header
- Key: X-API-Key
- Value: {{apiKey}}
```

**3. Bearer Token (JWT)**
```
Step 1: Get token from login endpoint
POST https://api.example.com/login
Body: {"username": "user", "password": "pass"}

Test script to save token:
const token = pm.response.json().token;
pm.environment.set('authToken', token);

Step 2: Use token in subsequent requests
Auth tab → Type: Bearer Token
Token: {{authToken}}

Or manually in headers:
Authorization: Bearer {{authToken}}
```

**4. Basic Auth**
```
Auth tab → Type: Basic Auth
Username: {{username}}
Password: {{password}}

Postman automatically Base64 encodes and adds:
Authorization: Basic dXNlcm5hbWU6cGFzc3dvcmQ=
```

**5. OAuth 2.0**
```
Auth tab → Type: OAuth 2.0
Click "Get New Access Token"
Configure:
- Token Name: MyToken
- Grant Type: Authorization Code / Client Credentials / etc.
- Callback URL: https://www.postman.com/oauth2/callback
- Auth URL: https://auth.example.com/authorize
- Access Token URL: https://auth.example.com/token
- Client ID: {{clientId}}
- Client Secret: {{clientSecret}}
- Scope: read write
- State: {{$guid}}

Click "Request Token" → Use Token
```

**6. Automated Token Refresh**
```javascript
// Pre-request script for automatic token refresh
const token = pm.environment.get('authToken');
const expiresAt = pm.environment.get('tokenExpiresAt');

// If token missing or expired, get new one
if (!token || !expiresAt || Date.now() > expiresAt) {
    console.log('Token expired, refreshing...');

    pm.sendRequest({
        url: pm.environment.get('authUrl') + '/token',
        method: 'POST',
        body: {
            mode: 'raw',
            raw: JSON.stringify({
                grant_type: 'refresh_token',
                refresh_token: pm.environment.get('refreshToken'),
                client_id: pm.environment.get('clientId'),
                client_secret: pm.environment.get('clientSecret')
            })
        }
    }, function(err, res) {
        if (!err) {
            const data = res.json();
            pm.environment.set('authToken', data.access_token);
            pm.environment.set('refreshToken', data.refresh_token);
            pm.environment.set('tokenExpiresAt', Date.now() + (data.expires_in * 1000));
            console.log('Token refreshed successfully');
        }
    });
}
```

**7. Handling Multi-step Auth**
```javascript
// Pre-request script for complex auth flows

// Step 1: Get CSRF token
pm.sendRequest(pm.environment.get('baseUrl') + '/csrf-token', (err, res) => {
    if (!err) {
        const csrfToken = res.json().csrfToken;
        pm.environment.set('csrfToken', csrfToken);

        // Step 2: Login with CSRF token
        pm.sendRequest({
            url: pm.environment.get('baseUrl') + '/login',
            method: 'POST',
            header: { 'X-CSRF-TOKEN': csrfToken },
            body: {
                mode: 'raw',
                raw: JSON.stringify({
                    username: pm.environment.get('username'),
                    password: pm.environment.get('password')
                })
            }
        }, (err2, res2) => {
            if (!err2) {
                const sessionToken = res2.json().sessionToken;
                pm.environment.set('sessionToken', sessionToken);
            }
        });
    }
});
```

**8. Secure Storage Best Practices**
```
✅ DO:
- Store credentials in environment variables
- Use Postman Vault for sensitive data
- Never commit environment files with secrets
- Use different credentials per environment
- Rotate secrets regularly

❌ DON'T:
- Hardcode tokens in requests
- Share collections with real secrets
- Log sensitive data
- Use same credentials everywhere
```

**Common variable issues and solutions:**

**1. Variable Not Resolving**
```
Symptoms: {{variable}} shows as text, not replaced

Check:
- Correct syntax: {{variableName}} (double curly braces)
- No spaces inside braces: {{variable}} ✓, {{ variable }} ✗
- Variable name spelled correctly
- Variable exists in current scope

Solutions:
- Hover over {{variable}} to see current value
- Check Environments tab (left sidebar)
- Verify environment is selected (top right dropdown)
- Use Quick Look (Ctrl+Alt+E / Cmd+Option+E)
```

**2. Wrong Variable Scope**
```
Variable precedence (highest to lowest):
1. Local variables (set in scripts with pm.variables.set())
2. Data variables (from iteration data files)
3. Collection variables
4. Environment variables
5. Global variables

If a variable isn't working, a higher scope may be overriding it.

Example:
// Environment has baseUrl = https://api.example.com
// Collection has baseUrl = http://localhost:3000
// {{baseUrl}} will use collection value (higher precedence)
```

**3. Variable Not Updating**
```javascript
// Correct way to set
pm.environment.set('token', newToken);

// Incorrect (won't work)
pm.environment.token = newToken;  // ❌

// Verify it set correctly
console.log(pm.environment.get('token'));

// Check if variable exists
if (pm.environment.has('token')) {
    console.log('Token exists');
}
```

**4. Environment Not Selected**
```
Symptom: Variables defined in environment aren't working

Check top right corner:
- Dropdown should show environment name (e.g., "Development")
- If it says "No Environment", no env vars are active

Solution: Select correct environment from dropdown
```

**5. Variable in Wrong Scope**
```javascript
// Setting in pre-request script
pm.variables.set('temp', 'value');  // Local - only available in this request
pm.environment.set('shared', 'value');  // Environment - available to all

// In test script
const val1 = pm.variables.get('temp');  // ✓ works
const val2 = pm.environment.get('shared');  // ✓ works
```

**6. Dynamic Variables Not Working**
```javascript
// Built-in variables work in URL/body
{{$guid}}  ✓
{{$timestamp}}  ✓
{{$randomInt}}  ✓

// In scripts, need replaceIn
const guid = pm.variables.replaceIn('{{$guid}}');  // ✓
const ts = pm.variables.replaceIn('{{$timestamp}}');  // ✓

// Custom dynamic
pm.variables.set('email', `user${Date.now()}@example.com`);  // ✓
```

**7. Variable Persistence Issues**
```javascript
// Variables persist during collection run
// Environment variables persist until changed
// Local variables reset after request

// To clear variables
pm.environment.unset('temp');
pm.collectionVariables.unset('temp');
pm.globals.unset('temp');
```

**8. Debugging Variables**
```javascript
// Log all variables
console.log('Environment:', pm.environment.toObject());
console.log('Collection:', pm.collectionVariables.toObject());
console.log('Globals:', pm.globals.toObject());

// Check specific variable
console.log('baseUrl:', pm.variables.get('baseUrl'));

// Quick Look shortcut
Ctrl+Alt+E / Cmd+Option+E
```

**Quick Checklist:**
□ Variable syntax correct ({{name}})
□ Environment selected in dropdown
□ Variable defined in correct scope
□ No higher scope overriding
□ No typos in name
□ Variable set correctly (using .set())
□ Check console for errors

**Complete Jenkins integration guide for Newman:**

**1. Basic Jenkins Pipeline**
```groovy
// Jenkinsfile
pipeline {
    agent any

    stages {
        stage('Checkout') {
            steps {
                checkout scm
            }
        }

        stage('Install Newman') {
            steps {
                sh 'npm install -g newman newman-reporter-htmlextra'
            }
        }

        stage('Run API Tests') {
            steps {
                sh '''
                    newman run collections/api-tests.postman_collection.json \
                        -e environments/staging.postman_environment.json \
                        -d data/test-data.csv \
                        --reporters cli,json,junit,htmlextra \
                        --reporter-json-export reports/results.json \
                        --reporter-junit-export reports/junit.xml \
                        --reporter-htmlextra-export reports/report.html
                '''
            }
        }

        stage('Publish Reports') {
            steps {
                junit 'reports/junit.xml'
                publishHTML([
                    reportDir: 'reports',
                    reportFiles: 'report.html',
                    reportName: 'API Test Report'
                ])
            }
        }
    }

    post {
